WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:01.480
Would you allow scientists

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:03.840
to expose you to the novel Coronavirus

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.010
if it would speed up vaccine development?

00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:09.630
Nearly 40,000 people from
more than 160 countries

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:12.900
have signed up to express interest in doing just that.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:16.200
As the world waits for the results of traditional vaccine trials,

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.180
in which participants come into contact

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.700
with the virus naturally,

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:24.980
human challenge trials would deliberately expose a much smaller sample of young, healthy volunteers,

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:27.960
potentially cutting the
study time by several months.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:32.280
But this approach is controversial among some public health officials and bioethicists,

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:37.380
because it would allow participants to put their own health at risk, without a foolproof rescue therapy.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.960
And this debate relates to a longstanding divide over whether people in low risk groups

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:47.280
should be allowed to jeopardize their own health to save the lives of others, and even get paid for doing so.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.900
To save one day, you know to me, ethically, that's a no brainer.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:57.160
Josh Morrison is a 34 year old former corporate lawyer, who founded the nonprofit 1 Day Sooner

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:59.360
to advocate for human challenge trials.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:03.880
If this approach speeds up
the availability of a vaccine, even by a single day,

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:06.040
Morrison argues it's worth pursuing.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:11.840
If you're moving the calendar forward by a day, we think that about 4500 lives around the world

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.940
is a reasonable starting point.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:20.140
And that obviously doesn't include things like, the economic activity, or the secondary consequences

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.300
on the kids who go back to school,

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:25.060
and the other illnesses that
people who are dying of those

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:27.500
because hospitals are
not able to treat them.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:29.700
Morrison got involved
with challenge trials

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.140
through his work trying to
solve the kidney shortage.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:34.260
He founded the nonprofit WaitList Zero

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.900
after donating a kidney himself to a stranger in 2011.

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:40.080
When the Coronavirus pandemic started,

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.360
Morrison recognized the parallels between challenge trials and kidney donations.

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:47.940
They're both ways for healthy volunteers to accept a small amount of risk

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:49.960
to save the lives of the more vulnerable.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:56.260
It's definitely a thing with real risks. At the same time, we let people do things that are uncertain all the time,

00:01:56.260 --> 00:02:01.460
and a lot of people say the risks to healthcare workers during the Coronavirus,

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:04.360
were uncertain, and still to some extent are uncertain.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:09.560
To the best of our knowledge with available data, those risks are gonna be lower than

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:10.920
other risks we let people take.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:14.160
Like childbirth, for example, or kidney donation.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.580
It's not just a matter of, well we think the
benefits exceed the risks,

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.700
and it's also not just a matter of, well it's the person's own choice.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:24.900
I think it's, I think that you need to hit both of those bars.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:30.440
But Dr. Anthony Fauci of The White House Coronavirus Task Force indicated in July that the government

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:34.500
has no plans to encourage or move forward
with its own challenge studies

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:39.680
since the current infection rate in the U.S. is high enough to conduct traditional trials quickly.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:41.820
Morrison says this view is shortsighted,

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:46.820
since challenge studies could still speed the development of multiple second generation vaccines,

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:52.180
that would be safer, more effective, and easier to distribute around the world than the first ones approved.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:56.400
He also thinks U.S. officials shouldn't be counting on high infection rates to continue.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.740
As an American, I think it would be a shame to just assume that people are gonna keep getting infected

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:03.560
at the rate they're getting infected now,

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:07.500
and that we can't accomplish what the rest of the developed world has accomplished.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:11.880
So I think that saying, oh, well, we don't need challenge studies because

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:14.000
we're pretty sure there's gonna be a lot of transmission,

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.540
and a lot of people in America are gonna get sick and die.

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:19.340
I think that seems misguided.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:22.680
Better to have it and not need it, than need it and not have it.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:28.120
Conducting a challenge trial also requires FDA approval, but an official at the agency said in July

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:31.140
that this approach could
cause quote ethical heartburn,

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:34.680
because if something bad happens, you don't have a perfect fix for it.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:39.260
Economists are accustomed to thinking about trade offs, it appears that at least

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:42.340
some parts of the ethics community, they are not.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:46.800
Dr. Alvin Roth is a Stanford economist who studies repugnant transactions,

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:50.620
or exchanges that many people don't think others should be allowed to make.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:54.960
He's one of fifteen Nobeloriate's who signed an open letter published by One Day Sooner,

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:58.840
urging the National Institutes of Health to prepare for challenge trials.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:03.700
Roth analogizes allowing well informed individuals to get infected with the coronavirus

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:06.400
to letting firefighters run
into a burning building.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:11.020
If you just wait 'til it burns itself out, you put lots of people at risk.

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:15.940
And so we pay firemen, to take additional risk, to go into the building.

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:19.520
He points out that traditional vaccine trials carry their own risks.

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:20.980
Unlike in challenge trials,

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:25.620
participants don't have the benefit of constant medical evaluation and first rate care.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:31.120
And in a normal trial, thousands of participants get a placebo, meaning they can both get infected,

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:32.580
and infect other people.

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:36.660
They may even engage in riskier behaviors if they think they've been vaccinated.

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:40.520
Challenge trials, particularly when there are multiple vaccines to be tested,

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:45.020
you could have the different vaccines be the conditions of the experiment.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:48.880
Instead of saying, some people get the vaccine, and some people don't.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:53.080
You could say, some people get vaccine A, and some people get vaccine B.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:59.180
Roth received his Nobel Prize in 2012, in part for his work applying market principles to kidney donations.

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:05.480
It's illegal to pay donors in the U.S., so he came up with way of encouraging them without direct compensation.

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:11.360
By using Roth's insights, policymakers were able to increase living donations by around 20%,

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:16.140
but tens of thousands of people still die each year in the U.S. due to the kidney shortage,

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:19.660
and Roth says that paying donors could save many more lives.

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:25.180
Though some fear that with this approach, poor or desperate people would be taken advantage of.

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:26.520
We pay people to become firemen,

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:30.020
who would not become firemen if we didn't pay them.

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:32.560
We should think about how to be appropriately grateful,

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:36.120
so that we're not exploiting the people who give us these things we need.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:39.700
I don't think we feel that we're exploiting our fire fighters.

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:44.880
Public service is something we do compensate people for, and we do wanna treat people well.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:49.920
And I think that is the commonality between kidney donation and being a challenge volunteer,

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:51.380
is this idea of public service,

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:58.060
where a lot of people see this idea of compensation, and they think that it's inherently exploitative,

00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:00.000
and that it's treating people as lesser.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.400
But it's actually the other way around.

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:07.120
With the world on hold waiting for results, thousands dying every day,

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:11.120
and hundreds of millions at risk of injury or poverty from the global pandemic.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:16.300
Researchers at Oxford University, who were the first to enter traditional human trials with a vaccine,

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:19.940
have had trouble recruiting enough participants who are likely to become infected,

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:23.320
as the natural virus transmission rate in the U.K. has fallen.

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:26.480
They are currently racing to prepare a protocol for challenge trials

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:29.480
that can pass muster with Oxford's ethical review board,

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:31.880
with the aim of beginning
them by the end of the year.

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:34.600
I'm confident it will be approved, yes.

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:40.500
In the U.S., Reuters reported in mid August that partly due to the public pressure applied by 1 Day Sooner,

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:42.800
government scientists were
just starting to prepare

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:48.440
a protocol for challenge trials, two months after the NIH first said it would start that process.

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:52.040
But the agency still has not committed to moving forward with challenge trials.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:55.900
And private efforts to develop a vaccine are following the government's lead.

00:06:55.900 --> 00:07:00.800
It's certainly ethical to move forward, and allow people like myself and the other people

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:06.740
who work at 1 Day Sooner, who want to take this risk and make the sacrifice to be able to do so.

